WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:01.918
In Top Gun: Maverick.

00:00:01.918 --> 00:00:03.628
All of this...

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:06.631
is real.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:08.758
These actors trained for months

00:00:08.758 --> 00:00:10.468
 to pull up to eight G's

00:00:10.468 --> 00:00:12.345
 And cameras mounted 
inside of the F-18s

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:15.015
captured real intense flying.

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:17.392
Just look at the ripples 
on Jay Ellis’ face.

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:20.395
But this shot is different

00:00:20.395 --> 00:00:23.064
because this plane doesn't exist.

00:00:23.982 --> 00:00:25.275
It's called The Darkstar

00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:27.235
and it uses hypersonic technology...

00:00:27.318 --> 00:00:29.863
a tool that's in development 
by Lockheed Martin

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:32.115
but nowhere near ready 
to be used like

00:00:32.115 --> 00:00:34.617
Tom  Cruise's Pete “Maverick” Mitchell 
does.

00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:38.079
This scene is almost 
entirely fabricated

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:41.666
from Tom Cruise's convincing sweating 
and heavy breathing

00:00:41.666 --> 00:00:44.085
to the impeccable VFX.

00:00:44.085 --> 00:00:46.212
But the thing that 
grounds it for me

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:47.630
is the sound design...

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:53.261
which is why I talked 
to this guy.

00:00:54.596 --> 00:00:55.555
I'm Al Nelson.

00:00:55.555 --> 00:01:00.477
My job on Top Gun: Maverick was 
to define the soundscape

00:01:00.477 --> 00:01:02.562
for the film, from jets...

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:04.230
to doors...

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:06.191
foley...

00:01:06.941 --> 00:01:07.776
ambiances...

00:01:08.818 --> 00:01:09.944
motorcycles...

00:01:10.987 --> 00:01:12.572
and all things maverick.

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:13.698
Expectations were high.

00:01:13.698 --> 00:01:14.949
The bar was very high.

00:01:14.949 --> 00:01:17.702
There wasn't a ‘good enough’ 
option.

00:01:17.786 --> 00:01:20.371
It always needed to be 
as best as it could be.

00:01:20.455 --> 00:01:22.707
When Al's team first got 
the mach 10 sequence...

00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:25.085
it sounded something like this:

00:01:26.544 --> 00:01:28.713
A blank slate with one big question.

00:01:29.339 --> 00:01:31.800
How do you make fake flight 
feel real?

00:01:32.342 --> 00:01:33.802
What does the Dark Star 
sound like?

00:01:33.802 --> 00:01:35.512
First of all, 
working backwards.

00:01:35.512 --> 00:01:36.930
It's not an F-18.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.848
It's not a fighter jet.

00:01:38.848 --> 00:01:41.267
The Darkstar is 
much more elegant.

00:01:41.309 --> 00:01:43.019
It's much more advanced.

00:01:43.019 --> 00:01:45.396
And one of the things 
that was important

00:01:45.396 --> 00:01:47.482
was for that to be 
believable as well.

00:01:47.482 --> 00:01:48.942
It shouldn't sound like a video game.

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:50.735
It can't sound sci-fi.

00:01:50.735 --> 00:01:52.529
You think, of course, 
of Star Wars

00:01:52.529 --> 00:01:55.740
you know, the tie fighters 
are amazing

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.033
but they're sci fi

00:01:57.033 --> 00:01:59.452
and they used elephants 
to make them.

00:02:03.581 --> 00:02:05.750
And so we worked hard, 
lots of field trips

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.211
to aircraft carriers, 
cross-country trips

00:02:08.211 --> 00:02:11.005
for jet engines and 
auxiliary power units.

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:15.260
We are trying to tell the story 
of Maverick's flight.

00:02:15.426 --> 00:02:17.637
Everything we're hearing
and seeing

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:20.181
should feel as real 
as it can feel

00:02:20.181 --> 00:02:22.767
so that we're experiencing 
this with him.

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:26.855
And Al’s job was to make sure that 
the experience captured

00:02:26.855 --> 00:02:29.107
all the intensity of Maverick pushing

00:02:29.107 --> 00:02:31.109
the Dark Star to its limits.

00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:35.071
It starts with sounds of technology
that we're familiar with.

00:02:35.238 --> 00:02:39.200
So when you see Maverick launch, 
he's using turbines.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:43.121
We wanted it to have punch 
and feel high tech.

00:02:43.121 --> 00:02:44.289
It doesn't ramp up.

00:02:44.289 --> 00:02:45.540
It just goes ‘ca-chunk’...

00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:46.833
and then thrust.

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.169
Maverick gets up in the air

00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:51.796
and as he starts passing 
new thresholds...

00:02:52.046 --> 00:02:53.590
“Increase to mach 3.5.”

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.466
...there's this subtle beep...
[movie clip beeps, high pitched]

00:02:55.466 --> 00:02:57.010
“Increase to mach 3.5.”

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:00.138
..that starts a simmering build of tension.

00:03:00.263 --> 00:03:01.806
Like Pavlov's dogs

00:03:01.806 --> 00:03:03.725
we're being trained to know 
that this tone

00:03:03.725 --> 00:03:04.851
will keep repeating 

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:06.978
until we get to the coveted mach 10.

00:03:07.645 --> 00:03:10.148
But before that, 
he has to go faster...

00:03:10.648 --> 00:03:13.776
this time using technology 
that most of us don't yet

00:03:13.776 --> 00:03:15.069
have a reference for.

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:16.988
“Transitioning to scramjet.”

00:03:17.822 --> 00:03:21.701
A scramjet uses 
the speed of the jet...

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:25.121
to intake oxygen, 
which ignites the fuel

00:03:25.121 --> 00:03:26.456
as opposed to a turbine.

00:03:26.915 --> 00:03:28.958
A turbine can only spin so fast

00:03:28.958 --> 00:03:30.043
and can only generate 

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:32.837
so much oxygen to 
ignite a fuel.

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:36.049
All of that process needed 
to be articulated sonically.

00:03:36.132 --> 00:03:37.550
He kicks it in the scramjet

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.886
and then you see 
the turbines close...

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:43.097
and you see the tube open up 
in the back of the Dark Star.

00:03:43.097 --> 00:03:45.767
At that point, you've got
this flow of air...

00:03:46.017 --> 00:03:49.896
going into the jet system and 
igniting that fuel...

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:52.106
and creating that rocket.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.194
And then the plane 
is on its way.

00:03:56.527 --> 00:03:58.029
Maverick starts getting 
comfortable.

00:03:58.029 --> 00:03:59.155
“We’re feeling good.”

00:03:59.697 --> 00:04:01.282
And the soundscape 
reflects that

00:04:01.282 --> 00:04:03.368
by becoming slightly more 
subdued.

00:04:03.368 --> 00:04:04.285
Because...

00:04:04.285 --> 00:04:06.621
The other thing we're doing
specifically with Dark Star

00:04:06.621 --> 00:04:08.915
is we are trying to tell the story

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:13.127
of his joy of being in this flight 
and achieving...

00:04:13.294 --> 00:04:15.546
that which no one has done before.

00:04:15.546 --> 00:04:17.006
“He's the fastest man alive.”

00:04:17.006 --> 00:04:18.466
And so there are moments

00:04:18.466 --> 00:04:21.344
where we're just with him 
as he's smiling.

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:24.013
The plane-focused sound design 
pulls back

00:04:24.013 --> 00:04:26.224
and other elements take 
the forefront.

00:04:26.474 --> 00:04:27.475
It's a lot of layers

00:04:27.475 --> 00:04:30.311
but it's also it's not 
necessarily accumulating.

00:04:30.728 --> 00:04:31.729
It's alternating.

00:04:31.729 --> 00:04:32.647
It's orchestrating.

00:04:32.647 --> 00:04:34.649
-You know, when he says...
-”Talk to me, Goose.”

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:37.360
there's not a lot else
going on

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.612
because we are 
with him emotionally.

00:04:39.821 --> 00:04:42.156
You see the Dark Star go off 
in the distance.

00:04:42.156 --> 00:04:44.158
That's very much 
a music moment.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.244
So you don't need a lot from us 
at that point.

00:04:46.244 --> 00:04:47.745
And we're just a dot

00:04:47.745 --> 00:04:50.081
and we're just a little rip 
as you hear it

00:04:50.081 --> 00:04:52.125
scan across the sky so 
you can track it 

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:56.004
and then the music drops out 
very dramatically 

00:04:56.004 --> 00:04:57.088
and we cut to him...

00:04:57.422 --> 00:04:58.339
...and it's boom.

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:00.675
And you feel a little bit 
of shaking

00:05:00.675 --> 00:05:02.385
and you hear that turbine 
kick on

00:05:02.385 --> 00:05:04.053
and you feel those thrusters.

00:05:04.053 --> 00:05:04.929
and you feel those thrusters.

00:05:05.263 --> 00:05:08.641
Instead of having all these clips
sound fade into each other...

00:05:08.766 --> 00:05:11.436
you feel every single cut.

00:05:15.148 --> 00:05:16.941
One of my first interactions 
with Tom

00:05:16.941 --> 00:05:19.777
he said “The cuts have to hit.
They have to punch.” 

00:05:19.777 --> 00:05:22.030
He was very, very emphatic 
about that.

00:05:22.196 --> 00:05:26.617
And it's a style that the first 
Top Gun established.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:31.748
That aggressive cutting, style of 
cutting from inside

00:05:31.748 --> 00:05:34.625
and being just dialogue 
and rather quiet

00:05:34.625 --> 00:05:37.003
to banging on to the exteriors.

00:05:37.462 --> 00:05:38.254
“Coming left!”

00:05:39.547 --> 00:05:41.799
And it makes the cut feel aggressive.

00:05:41.799 --> 00:05:44.177
It makes the film feel aggressive 
and dangerous

00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:46.220
and it propels us in the story.

00:05:46.429 --> 00:05:49.015
Each of these cuts has a 
unique sonic texture.

00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:51.601
You cut to the rear and 
it just bangs on

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:54.854
with this ripping, tearing 
rocket thruster.

00:05:55.772 --> 00:05:57.857
You see all of those currents

00:05:57.857 --> 00:06:00.068
crossing the wings, 
the jet stream.

00:06:00.068 --> 00:06:02.028
It's such a beautiful visual.

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:04.030
And we wanted to put 
something in there

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:05.865
that was tonal and special

00:06:05.865 --> 00:06:08.493
which was the Roebling 
Suspension Bridge.

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:12.538
So all of these flavors

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:16.125
allow it to be constantly changing 
and constantly new...

00:06:16.584 --> 00:06:18.669
and hopefully exciting.

00:06:19.587 --> 00:06:21.547
To ratchet up that 
building tension

00:06:21.547 --> 00:06:24.342
Al’s team used a longtime 
sound design trick...

00:06:24.675 --> 00:06:27.136
the Shepard Tone:
an auditory illusion

00:06:27.136 --> 00:06:30.098
where you loop a sound wave 
separated by octaves...

00:06:30.264 --> 00:06:31.933
which tricks your brain 
into thinking

00:06:31.933 --> 00:06:34.060
that it's a continually 
rising pitch.

00:06:34.394 --> 00:06:36.854
Listen to it here in 
the rising turbines.

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:43.486
So the jet is starting to
complain a little bit

00:06:43.486 --> 00:06:45.279
and so more tones are happening.

00:06:45.279 --> 00:06:48.991
And yes, that 
mach 9.7, 9.8...

00:06:49.242 --> 00:06:51.494
each time it's a little bit louder, 
a little bit higher.

00:06:51.536 --> 00:06:53.538
It's that winding you up.

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:55.706
And then the minute he does it...

00:06:56.416 --> 00:06:58.084
“Mach 10! 
[cheers]”

00:06:58.084 --> 00:06:59.460
Catharsis.

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:03.881
But just when you think 
it's over...

00:07:05.007 --> 00:07:06.926
we return to quiet.

00:07:06.926 --> 00:07:09.178
And then we start again 
with the build.

00:07:09.178 --> 00:07:10.680
“Oh, don't do it. 
Don't do it.”

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:15.643
“Just... a little... push.”

00:07:15.643 --> 00:07:17.770
But this time the build 
is different.

00:07:18.104 --> 00:07:20.398
It starts with this beep from inside 
the control room...

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:23.609
which plays off this last beep 
from the Dark Star's cockpit.

00:07:24.068 --> 00:07:26.904
When you listen to them both 
next to each other

00:07:26.904 --> 00:07:29.449
it's a rising tone and then 
a falling tone.

00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:33.327
And this subtly signals that 
danger is coming.

00:07:35.538 --> 00:07:37.331
The shepherd tone kicks 
in louder

00:07:37.331 --> 00:07:39.125
and more grating 
than before.

00:07:39.125 --> 00:07:41.002
It leaks into the sound-muted cabin.

00:07:41.002 --> 00:07:43.212
So we know that the pressure 
is building...

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:45.715
and it keeps building until...

00:07:48.176 --> 00:07:49.260
[Explosion]

00:07:52.096 --> 00:07:53.556
But you know, it took 
some hard work

00:07:53.556 --> 00:07:55.850
to get us to this final version.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:57.810
Did some late nights 
and long hours

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.145
but I'm very pleased

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:01.230
and excited with with how 
it's been received

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:02.440
and how it sounds.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:08.154
I know they did design an actual 
Dark Star that he sat in.

00:08:08.154 --> 00:08:09.447
You can see him sitting in it

00:08:09.447 --> 00:08:11.240
and then at a certain point 
it launches over our head

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:13.075
and that's when we get into 
the magic of cinema.

00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:17.622
That was an F-18 that they
then remapped the Dark Star over it.

00:08:17.622 --> 00:08:19.457
And that jet was so low

00:08:19.457 --> 00:08:20.750
that when you see the roof

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:23.544
of that shed blow off, 
that was real.

00:08:23.544 --> 00:08:26.506
That was an unexpected addition.

00:08:27.089 --> 00:08:30.551
And the fact that Ed Harris 
just stands there and takes it...

00:08:31.844 --> 00:08:33.513
like that guy is

00:08:33.513 --> 00:08:34.639
he’s something else.

